(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #111 closes the #109 report's "new, confirmed hazard":
 under the old pointer-in nesting, `Free outer.` here deep-freed the SAME
 block `inner` still named, and `print inner's length.` afterward
 segfaulted (rc 139, reproduced on this branch before #111). Copy-in means
 `outer` never held `inner`'s own block in the first place - it held an
 independent copy from the moment `outer` was built - so freeing `outer`
 leaves `inner` fully valid.)

a list called inner is [1, 2, 3].
a list called outer is [inner, "x"].
Free outer.
print inner's length.
print inner.
